This paper introduces and analyzes a stochastic Newton approach designed for high-dimensional distributed stochastic convex optimization. In this framework, the objective function is expressed as a weighted average of local components held by multiple agents, where weights are uniform in the ideal scenario but subject to noise in practical applications. The proposed algorithm enables these agents to collaboratively compute a solution without revealing their local functions to one another. By leveraging local Hessian information, the method achieves a convergence rate comparable to centralized stochastic Newton techniques. Furthermore, the study establishes a communication complexity of $O(\log(1/\epsilon))$ required to reach an $\epsilon$-accurate solution, assuming ideal weights. This result holds for both bounded domain and bounded gradient variance conditions, covering both smooth and non-smooth convex objectives. Additionally, the paper presents a numerical validation of the proposed approach. Specifically, the primary contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[\textbf{(A)}] We introduce a distributed Newton method capable of achieving a $\epsilon$-accurable solution in $O(\log(1/\epsilon))$ communication rounds, a result that is optimal for centralized stochastic Newton methods.
    \item[\textbf{(B)}] We provide a rigorous analysis of the proposed algorithm, demonstrating that it attains a $\epsilon$-accurable solution with $N$ samples and $M$ agents under conditions of identical distribution, bounded domains, and bounded gradient variance.
    \item[\textbf{(C)}] We present a numerical evaluation of the proposed approach.
\end{itemize}
Additionally, we address the scenario involving non-identical distribution (non-IID data). Specifically, we demonstrate that the algorithm remains effective in this setting, though it necessitates an increased number of samples per agent. The primary contributions are as follows:
\begin{itemize}
    \item[\textbf{(A)}] We introduce a Newton-based algorithm that achieves a $\epsilon$-accurable solution with $O(\log(1/\epsilon))$ communication rounds, matching the optimal convergence rate of centralized stochastic Newton methods.
    \item[\textbf{(B)}] We provide a rigorous analysis of the proposed algorithm, establishing that it reaches a $\epsilon$-accurable solution using $N$ samples and $M$ agents, even under non-IID data conditions.
    \item[\textbf{(C)}] We present a numerical evaluation of the proposed approach.
\end{itemize}

